  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A couple of slow moving to stationary boundaries\nhave been evident on satellite since this morning, and along\nthese boundaries scattered showers are ongoing. Dry air at low\nlevels (700-800mb) and at mid levels (~500mb) as denoted on the\n12Z MHX sounding should help keep showers shallow and brief in \nnature, and as a result thunder chances remain low through \ntoday. With a loss of daytime heating once the sun sets,\ncoverage of shower should come down drastically.\n\nWe are pretty warm with temps in the low 80s inland, mid 70s\nalong the coast. Highs shouldn't get much higher than this,\nespecially with cloud cover increasing inland. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Friday - Still looks like widespread showers \nand a few thunderstorms breaking out during the afternoon into \nevening as a cold front moves through Eastern NC. Ahead of the\nfront, we could see more isolated to scattered showers moving SW\nto NE inland in the morning hours, still uncertain how much of \nan impact these have to instability later on in the day.\nCircling back to Friday afternoon, anomalous moisture and \nhigher-than-normal instability (assuming morning showers have\nlittle to no impacT) for early November is expected, as \nindicated by EFI highlighting CAPE vals nearing the 80th \npercentile. Have maintained thunder threat for inland areas \nwhere the EFI is highlighting the higher CAPE/Shear indices \ninland, and where highest instability and shear will reside. \nConfidence is increasing on the best forcing lining up with peak\nheating, a strong to severe thunderstorm threat would \nmaterialize for areas especially along and west of the\nJacksonville/New Bern/Washington, NC corridor in the afternoon\nand early evening Main threat would be damaging wind gusts. With\nsome weak helicity present a brief, weak tornado can't be ruled\nout. The front sweeps offshore late Friday with any remaining \nshowers exiting the OBX around or shortly after midnight Friday\nnight.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Good agreement amongst models with frontal\npassage late Friday, allowing a more seasonably dry airmass to\nusher in for the weekend. Return to mostly sunny skies and \nhighs in the upper 60s to low 70s Sat, then cooler yet for Sun \nwith 60-65. Lows will be in the 40s, with some 30s for some of \nthe normally colder spots. We will have to monitor the temp\nforecast for any frost concerns Sunday morning.",
